# Intent: a fallback hub endpoint for both channels so the robot works away from home without any public address (owner 2026-11-14: Jesse takes the robot to a friend's house). The voice and avatar channels try the saved home address as today; when it cannot be reached (two failed attempts, or a plain TCP probe that gets no answer in 2 s: the stock socket connect has no timeout) they try ws://<STACKCHAN_FALLBACK_HUB_HOST>:<the same port> with the same saved pairing token (nothing is written to NVS), then retry the home address about once a minute (and probe it while connected on the fallback) so the unit returns home by itself. The default fallback host is 192.168.2.1, the fixed address macOS Internet Sharing gives the laptop on its own Wi-Fi, where a small relay forwards the two ports over Tailscale to the hub. The list is open for a third (public wss) entry: one more row in main/hal/maipai_fallback.cc and one more state in the two connect loops. No Offline label while a fallback attempt succeeds (the label counts from the last connected moment).
# Check after a flash: on the home network the serial log shows no fallback; with the home address unreachable and a relay on the fallback host the log says "Home address failed twice: trying the fallback endpoint" then "Hub reached through the fallback endpoint"; back on the home network it says "Home address answers again: going back" within about a minute.
